// SPDX-License-Identifier: LGPL-3.0-or-later
//
// @FR-F-Spec — `{p:j}`, `{p:J}` and `{p:json}` are one decision, so they are one answer.
//
// The spec parser asks two questions in a row: *does a WIDTH expression start here* and *which
// radix does this letter name*.  They are the same question — the first is the second's domain
// — and they were answered from two separate lists.  The radix reader accepted `J` and `json`
// (it lower-cased before comparing); the skip list named only `j`.  So for every spelling but
// `j` the width expression consumed the letter:
//
//   * with no variable of that name, an "Unknown variable 'json'" that never mentions the spec;
//   * with one — and `json` is an ordinary name for a variable in a program that renders JSON —
//     that variable's value silently became the WIDTH, the radix reverted to decimal, and the
//     hole rendered the compact loft form instead of the JSON the author asked for.
//
// The second is why this file binds `json` rather than leaving it undeclared: the undeclared
// case is loud, and the loud case is not the one that costs anything.
//
// Axes this file MOVES:   the spelling of the JSON radix, and whether a variable shadows it.
// Axes it HOLDS FIXED:    the hole's type is a struct.  `:j` on an INTEGER is a refusal and
//                         lives with the other refusals, in
//                         `a-format-spec-the-renderer-cannot-execute-is-refused.loft`.
//
// @falsified-at: d9aa10ac — interpret exit 1 -> 0, native exit 1 -> 0.  `asserts` reads 0 on
// the control on purpose: there the file does not COMPILE, so the refusal is the channel and
// no assertion is reached.  That is why these cells are not in the runtime guard beside them.
//   HOLDS: assertions, leak, panic, free-refusal, expectations are equal on both trees.
//   Falsify prints only the channels that MOVE and requires HERE to be clean, so each of
//   those is clean on both. The native side was not scored in this sweep.

struct JsP { js_r: integer, js_g: integer }

fn main() {
  json = 9;
  p = JsP { js_r: 1, js_g: 2 };
  // The control: the one spelling that was always right.
  j1 = "{p:j}";
  assert(j1 == "{{\"js_r\":1,\"js_g\":2}}", "control, :j renders JSON: {j1}");
  j2 = "{p:J}";
  assert(j2 == j1, "the :J spelling agrees with :j: {j2}");
  j3 = "{p:json}";
  assert(j3 == j1, "the :json spelling agrees with :j: {j3}");
  // The shadowed name is still the author's variable, not a width.
  assert(json == 9, "the json variable is not consumed by the spec: {json}");
}
